content.text:
The leak texts don't look the way I expected. Inside thinking, P(`<|im_end|>`) is diffuse (summed 0.04 to 1.9 over 1,400 to 4,000 tokens, and 0.01 to 0.18 at the stop that was actually sampled). P(`</think>`) is about 0 throughout. So these runs never get a chance to close their thinking. A new hypothesis: F1's gate shift makes detector activations more negative in non-repetitive thinking (the negative silu lobe), and a negative drive writes −v15, which suppresses `</think>`. The thinking then runs long, and because the presence penalty pushes down every token already used while `<|im_end|>` stays unpenalized, `<|im_end|>` eventually gets sampled. To test this, I'll first list the helper functions and arrays in the kernel. Then I can compare the drive distribution in thinking between P2 (start) and P4 (F1) on the same tokens.

```python
# Hypothesis: helper functions for drive c(t) and the P2/P4 activation arrays are still in the kernel; list them with shapes.
fns = sorted([k for k in ks if callable(globals()[k]) and not isinstance(globals()[k], type) and not k.startswith('_')])
print(fns)
for k in ['MA2','MA4','LP2','LP3','LP4','HS2','TKI2','TKL2','TKI4','TKL4','DET','BN','DIRS','P2_lens','P2_items','evalI','fitI','LIDX','HIDX','Eidx','A','B','C','D','E']:
    v = globals().get(k)
    if v is None: print(k,'None'); continue
    if hasattr(v,'shape'): print(k, type(v).__name__, v.shape, v.dtype)
    elif isinstance(v,(list,tuple,dict)): print(k, type(v).__name__, len(v), (list(v.items())[:3] if isinstance(v,dict) else v[:6]))
    else: print(k, type(v).__name__, str(v)[:100])
```

That output was too long because `P2_items` holds whole transcripts. From now on I'll print only lengths. Next I need the exact definitions of my existing helpers (`drives`, `region_masks`, `regions`) so I can reuse them on P2 and P4. I'll print their source, cut short.

```python
# Hypothesis: reuse existing helpers; print their source (truncated) to recall the drive definition and region masks.
import inspect
for fnm in ['drives','drives_on','region_masks','regions','detacts','c_mod']:
    try:
        src = inspect.getsource(globals()[fnm])
        print('-----',fnm, len(src)); print(src[:900])
    except Exception as ex: print(fnm, 'ERR', ex)
```

The helpers were defined inside executed cells, so their source can't be recovered. Their signatures, local variable names and constants still tell me what they compute. I'll print those, so I can reuse the functions without re-deriving the drive definition.

```python
# Hypothesis: signatures/varnames of helper functions reveal their inputs (enough to reuse them safely).
for fnm in ['drives','drives_on','region_masks','regions','detacts','c_mod','mlp_in','region_stats','joint','scan_dev2']:
    f_ = globals()[fnm]
    try:
        co = f_.__code__
        print(fnm, inspect.signature(f_), '| vars:', co.co_varnames[:18], '| consts:', [c for c in co.co_consts if isinstance(c,(int,float,str)) and not (isinstance(c,str) and len(c)>40)][:14])
    except Exception as ex: print(fnm,'ERR',ex)
```

F1 does not suppress `</think>` at natural thinking ends. Summed P(`</think>`) in thinking even rises (A 3.2→4.4, B 2.4→2.8), and the log-probability of the real `</think>` token is mixed (A −1.65→−1.78, D −1.40→−0.94). Only the 1% lower tail of the drive gets more negative (−0.36→−0.58). So the "negative lobe suppresses closing" hypothesis is not supported. In the P6 leak texts, P(`</think>`) is about 0 throughout. Most of those runs (loop_stop) are degenerate counting enumerations that would probably fail under either model.

Before investing more passes in the leak, I want to know what F1 actually costs on instruction following. P5 showed losses on highlight (−29), bullets (−19), title and lowercase. I'll split those losses into prompts lost only through a missing `</think>` and prompts lost even though both models answered. First I'll check what the P5 result variables hold.

```python
# Hypothesis: P5 per-kind losses (highlight, bullets, title, lower) are partly no-</think>; split them using only prompts both models answered.
for k in ['G5s','G5e','R5s','R5e','byid5s','byid5e','M5s','M5e','TASKS','IF_S']:
    v = globals().get(k)
    if hasattr(v,'shape'): print(k, v.shape)
    elif isinstance(v,dict): 
        kk = list(v.keys())[:2]; print(k,'dict',len(v), kk, type(v[kk[0]]).__name__, (list(v[kk[0]].keys())[:12] if isinstance(v[kk[0]],dict) else str(v[kk[0]])[:150]))
    elif isinstance(v,list): print(k,'list',len(v), type(v[0]).__name__, (list(v[0].keys())[:14] if isinstance(v[0],dict) else str(v[0])[:150]))
    else: print(k, type(v).__name__)
```

The (1+w) convention reproduces the recorded log-probabilities (error 0.04, float16 rounding), while plain w is off by 1.6. Now the attribution. In P6 I'll split the final logit difference logit(`<|im_end|>`) − logit(`</think>`) exactly into: everything before layer 14, layer 14 as a whole, and the attention and MLP outputs of layers 15 to 23, each scaled by the final-norm factor at that position. I'll compare three groups:
- G1: high-drive positions in leak drafts, where `<|im_end|>` wins;
- G2: low-drive normal thinking;
- G3: high-drive exit points in the synthetic loops, where `</think>` wins.

The components whose contribution differs most between G1 and G3 are the candidates for routing the loop exit to the wrong end token.

```python
# Hypothesis: a few late components (attn or MLP in layers 15-23) route the E2 'end' push to im_end in draft contexts vs </think> in loop contexts.
WD = {l: load(f'model.language_model.layers.{l}.mlp.down_proj.weight').numpy() for l in range(15,24)}
dvec_it = Emb[IM_END]-Emb[THINK_END]
def dla_text(i, pos):
    h = HS6[i][:, pos].astype(np.float32)       # (11, P, 1024) hidden 14..24
    a = MA6[i][:, pos].astype(np.float32)       # (9, P, 3584) layers 15..23
    s = (1+wf)[None,:]/np.sqrt((h[10]**2).mean(1,keepdims=True)+1e-6)   # (P,1024)
    comps = {'pre14': h[0], 'L14': h[1]-h[0]}
    for j,l in enumerate(range(15,24)):
        mo = a[j]@WD[l].T
        comps[f'm{l}'] = mo; comps[f'a{l}'] = (h[j+2]-h[j+1]) - mo
    return {k:((v*s)@dvec_it) for k,v in comps.items()}
groups = {'G1_leak_hi':[], 'G2_leak_lo':[], 'G3_loop_exit':[], 'G4_loop_hi_mid':[]}
for i in range(11):
    n=L6[i]; s0=TS6[i]; pos=np.arange(s0, n-1); ce=CE6[i][pos]
    if i<9:
        sel={'G1_leak_hi':pos[ce>0.75], 'G2_leak_lo':pos[ce<0.25]}
    else:
        pte=PTE6[i][pos]; sel={'G3_loop_exit':pos[(ce>0.75)&(pte>0.05)], 'G4_loop_hi_mid':pos[(ce>0.75)&(pte<0.01)]}
    for g,pp_ in sel.items():
        if len(pp_)==0: continue
        if len(pp_)>600: pp_=np.sort(np.random.default_rng(0).choice(pp_,600,replace=False))
        groups[g].append(dla_text(i,pp_))
res = {}
for g,lst in groups.items():
    res[g] = {k: np.concatenate([d[k] for d in lst]).mean() for k in lst[0]}
    res[g]['n'] = sum(len(d['pre14']) for d in lst)
dfD = pd.DataFrame(res).T
cols = ['n','pre14','L14']+[f'{x}{l}' for l in range(15,24) for x in ('a','m')]
dfD['total']=dfD[[c for c in cols if c!='n']].sum(1)
print(dfD[cols+['total']].round(2).T.to_string())
```
